  "title": "Emah",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/temple-2"
  ],
  "details": "<p>This 1750 m\u00b2 temple was excavated by <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hormuzd_Rassam\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Hormuzd Rassam</a> in 1880, <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Koldewey\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Robert Koldewey</a> in 1900, and Iraqi archaeologists in 1961\u201362.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The temple of the goddess Ninmah/B\u0113let-il\u012b, Emah (\u201cExalated House\u201d), was located in the Ka-dingirra district (modern Kasr) of Babylon. According to the Babylonian topographical text Tintir = Babylon Tablet IV, this was one of four temples in that part of the city, which was located in the northern part of the eastern half of Babylon. Emah was rebuilt by the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal (r. 668\u2013ca. 631 BC) and the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar II (r. 604\u2013562 BC).",
